{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`ready_tickets` is bounded at 90,000 bytes by bug `daughterly-agitative-ocelot`\n(`494b-2dd3-e9d3-4fb0`), but even after that fix's lean projection it sits at **58 rows /\n87,893 wire bytes — roughly ONE row of headroom**. The bound is correct and its refusal is\nactionable (it points at `next_batch`), but the tool will begin refusing its default call\nabout as soon as one more ticket becomes ready. That is a cliff, not a fixed problem.\n\n`ready_tickets` is a discovery surface: it answers \"what can I work on next?\" A discovery\ncaller needs id, alias, title, type, status, priority, and blocking summary, not dependency\narrays, file-impact arrays, authorship, comments, or full ticket defaults. Measured on the real\nstore (58 ready rows, 90,000-byte budget):\n\n| shape | bytes/row | rows that fit | headroom vs today |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| as shipped (lean, `TicketStateOut`) | 1,515 | 59 | +1 |\n| lean minus `file_impact`/`deps`/author/env fields | 801 | 112 | +54 |\n| summary fields, still via `TicketStateOut` | 572 | 157 | +99 |\n| summary fields via a NEW closed output model | 234 | 384 | +326 |\n\nTwo findings drive this:\n\n1. **The lean ready row still carries fields a discovery caller cannot use.** `file_impact` is\n   252 B/row (18.1% of the row) and `deps` 160 B/row (11.5%) — 30% of the payload between them,\n   for a question that only needs discovery fields and blocking state.\n2. **`TicketStateOut`'s declared defaults set a floor.** Projecting to summary fields but\n   returning `TicketStateOut` still costs 572 B/row, because `model_validate` re-adds\n   `description: null`, `comments: []`, `deps: []`, `inbound_deps: []`, `file_impact: []`,\n   `file_impact_scope`, `no_file_impact_reason`, `plan_review_health: null` and\n   `cross_session_warning: null`. **338 of those 572 bytes are re-added defaults** — the same\n   mechanism that made the parent bug's budget under-estimate by 178%.\n\n## Approach\n\nImplement the durable fix: change the default `ready_tickets(full=False)` MCP response to a new\nclosed discovery output model with exactly the fields needed for choosing work (ticket id, alias,\ntitle, type, status, priority, and blocking summary). Keep `ready_tickets(full=True)` returning the\nexisting full `TicketStateOut` shape for callers that explicitly opt in to full details. This\nmirrors common actively maintained OSS API practice: list/discovery endpoints return compact item\nsummaries, while detail or explicit-expanded calls return the complete resource.\n\nDo not change `list_tickets` in this ticket. Its filters make an over-budget refusal actionable,\nso the budget is a guardrail there; `ready_tickets` has no narrowing arguments and needs a safer\ndefault shape. Document the output narrowing as its own release note and changelog entry because a\nnew output schema is a declared contract change rather than just another field omission under the\n`494b` axis.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_mcp_reads.py` — select the discovery model for default `ready_tickets` and retain\n  the full model when `full=True`.\n- `src/rebar/_mcp_models.py` — define the closed discovery output model/schema.\n- `src/rebar/_engine_support/reads.py` — adjust only if the shared read projection needs a\n  matching discovery field set.\n- `tests/interfaces/facades/test_mcp_list_payload_budget.py` — add the per-row-size regression.\n- `docs/release-notes.md` and `CHANGELOG.md` — document the default response narrowing and the\n  unchanged `full=True` escape hatch.\n\n## Testing\n\n- RED first: add a regression in `tests/interfaces/facades/test_mcp_list_payload_budget.py` that\n  fails on current `main` because default `ready_tickets` rows exceed the discovery per-row ceiling\n  after `model_validate` and `js_safe_result`.\n- Happy path: default `ready_tickets` returns discovery-shaped rows and the measured wire payload has\n  at least 3x current-store headroom.\n- Compatibility path: `ready_tickets(full=True)` still returns full `TicketStateOut` rows with fields\n  such as `deps`/`file_impact` available.\n- Documentation: release notes and changelog name the contract change and the `full=True` escape\n  hatch.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] A decision is recorded on the ticket to use a new closed discovery output model for default\n      `ready_tickets`, with `full=True` continuing to return full `TicketStateOut` rows.\n- [ ] Default `ready_tickets` has headroom for at least 3x the current ready count on this store,\n      measured on the wire payload after `model_validate` and `js_safe_result`; verified by a\n      regression test in `tests/interfaces/facades/test_mcp_list_payload_budget.py`.\n- [ ] The output-schema change is recorded in `docs/release-notes.md` and `CHANGELOG.md`, including\n      the unchanged `full=True` escape hatch.\n- [ ] `list_tickets` is explicitly left unchanged because its filter arguments make over-budget\n      refusal actionable; this asymmetry is documented in the change.\n- [ ] Regression coverage asserts the per-row size ceiling so default ready rows cannot silently\n      re-grow."}},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1788806249211686256,"uuid":"06ecdb41-57c3-4d71-aa4f-ff21731c98bf"}